An instrument to find out where the missing cameras are

The phone has four rear cameras. Two work; the wide angle (Hi-1337), the macro
(Hi-259) and the depth one hang off cci1_i2c0, which in the device tree is
EMPTY. To write a driver for them, two pieces of information are needed that
nobody states: at which address they answer and with which GPIO their reset is
released.

WHY SCANNING FROM OUTSIDE IS NOT ENOUGH

Measured: with the rear camera powered, four chips answer on that bus -0x51,
0x54, 0x59 and 0x5c- but NONE of them responds to the SK Hynix identification
register. The mainline hi846 driver says that register is 0x0f16 and that it
returns 46 08; a Hi-1337 would have to return 37 13. What is read is ff ff, or
nothing.

So the sensors are powered down. And turning rails on by hand is not enough: a
sensor also needs its MCLK -which only a driver brings up- and to have its reset
released.

WHAT THIS MODULE DOES

It runs the full sequence just as a real driver would -supplies, clock, wait- and
then, for each candidate GPIO:

    releases it, sweeps the whole bus, and puts it back

For every address that answers, it reads the Hynix identifier (0x0f16) and the
CCS standard one (0x0000), which is where Sony and Samsung put it. That way a
single test answers both questions at once: who is there and with which GPIO it
wakes up.

It starts by sweeping WITHOUT releasing any reset, which is the reference
everything else is compared against.

DECISIONS THAT MATTER

  - It goes in as a MODULE and is loaded by hand. A failure here cannot leave the
    phone unable to boot, which has already happened in this project with the
    audio macros.
  - It always returns -ENODEV. It is an instrument, not a driver: it measures and
    it leaves.
  - The GPIOs are requested at rest (active low, that is, the sensor IN reset) so
    as not to wake anything up prematurely.
  - It sits at address 0x7f, which nobody uses, so as not to collide with the
    four chips that already answer.

WHAT WAS ASSUMED, and how it turned out

  - MCLK2 on gpio15: IT WAS THE WRONG CLOCK. The right one is MCLK3 on gpio16,
    and the clock runs at 19.2 MHz and not at 24. Both facts come from the
    module's own factory blob. Now MCLK2 and MCLK3 are brought up together.
  - The reset: CONFIRMED, it is tlmm 24 (CAM_RESET3 in the factory data).
    Releasing it makes a chip appear at 0x21 that is not on the bus beforehand.
  - The candidate GPIOs: the list now comes from the phone's own pin ownership
    table, not from guessing. The reset goes first, followed by 11 STANDBY
    candidates, because the blob says this sensor needs a standby and that it
    goes BEFORE the reset.

If the sensor does not wake up with that, the sweep itself says so and other
combinations are tried. It is cheap: you do not need to get it right first time,
you need to be able to tell the difference.

THE FIRST STANDBY ROUND: NEGATIVE, AND SHORTER THAN IT LOOKED

The 11 tlmm candidates that were tried first all came out negative: nine were
exercised and 0x21 kept reading 0000 at 0x0f16 on all nine, and the other two
-tlmm 26 and 37- give -EIO when requested as outputs, which is what
gpiod_direction_output() returns when the pin is tied to an interrupt. They are
the IRQs of the vibration chip (aw8695, at 1-005a) and of NFC (nxp-nci_i2c), so
those two cannot be a camera standby and are ruled out on principle.

BUT that was NOT a complete sweep, even though it was taken as one. Counting the
tlmm pins with no owner on this phone gives 83, not 11: that list covered 13%.
It had been chosen by proximity to the camera pins, which is a reasonable
heuristic, but nobody wrote it down as an assumption and it ended up being read
as "exhausted". The lesson is about measurement, not about the kernel: when
declaring a sweep complete you have to say over which universe, and count it.

This version widens it to the 19 free tlmm pins below 59 and adds the 16 free
GPIOs of the two PMICs, which had never been looked at.

And with the widened list, still nothing: 28 candidates exercised -19 from the
tlmm and 11 from the PMICs- and 0x21 still reads 0000 at 0x0f16 on all 28. Of the
PMIC GPIOs, five give -EPERM, which is what pinctrl-spmi-gpio returns when the
pin does not belong to the application processor but to another subsystem.

THE STANDBY PREMISE WAS FALSE, AND THAT VOIDS ALL THREE ROUNDS

The whole standby sweep was born from reading a type 9 step -SENSOR_STANDBY- in
the Hi-1337 blob and deducing that a pin had to be driven before the reset. The
deduction does not hold up, and the counterexample was right at home:

  - The IMX682 (rear) WORKS in mainline, and its node only has
    reset-gpios = <&tlmm 25>. No standby. Its factory blob DOES carry a
    STANDBY=1 step at the end.
  - The S5K3T2 (front) WORKS, and its node only has
    reset-gpios = <&tlmm 23>. No standby. Its blob DOES carry a STANDBY=0 at the
    start.

So on this phone a STANDBY step in the blob does NOT imply a pin that has to be
moved for the sensor to answer over i2c. The two cameras that work prove it.
Looking for that pin was chasing a ghost, and it explains why three sweep rounds
gave exactly the same zero.

The lesson, which is the usual one in this project: when you deduce something
from a data source, you have to test the deduction against a case whose answer
you already know. There were two of them right at hand.

ALSO RULED OUT: THE CLOCK DOES REACH THE PAD

It was suspected that the MCLK was being generated but not muxed to the pin. That
is not it: with the module loaded, pinmux-pins shows pins 15 and 16 in the hands
of 14-007f with function cam_mclk. The clock arrives.

ALSO RULED OUT: THE 16-BIT READ IS CORRECT

leer16() sends the register address high byte first and reassembles the same way,
which is what Hynix, Sony and Samsung require. And it is validated by the bus
itself: the two EEPROMs return different, sensible values that way (0101 and 0107
at 0x0000). When 0x21 returns 0000, it really does return 0000.

CORRECTION: THERE ARE NOT FOUR CHIPS ON THAT BUS, THERE ARE THREE

Above it says four answer -0x51, 0x54, 0x59 and 0x5c-. Measured afterwards: 0x59
and 0x5c are NOT devices. Dumping them, their entire map is the same 16 bytes
repeated on every row, and the content is a function of the address itself (0x09
for 0x59, 0x0c for 0x5c, that is, address & 0x0f). Moreover 0x59 = 0x51 + 8 and
0x5c = 0x54 + 8: they are echoes of the two EEPROMs. The real inventory of the
bus is: two EEPROMs (0x51 and 0x54) and the chip at 0x21 that appears with the
reset released. Sitting at 0x7f is still a good idea anyway.

TWO FAULTS THAT HUNG THE BOOT, AND THEIR FIX

This is the corrected version. The first one left the phone unable to boot for
two different reasons, and the second is the one that really explained it.

1. MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, ...) made udev load the module BY ITSELF, as soon as
   the node appeared. So 'it goes in as a module and is loaded by hand' was
   false: the probe ran at boot, unsupervised, turning rails on, muxing gpio15
   and wiggling seven GPIOs of unknown ownership. With that table removed no
   modalias is generated and udev does not load it; the of_match_table still
   serves to bind when you modprobe.

2. The node carried assigned-clocks / assigned-clock-rates on CAMCC_MCLK2_CLK.
   That is NOT applied by the driver: it is applied by the core when REGISTERING
   the node, in of_clk_set_defaults(), with no driver involved. The frequency is
   now set with clk_set_rate() inside the probe.

HOW THEY ARE TOLD APART, which is what I was missing: the postmarketOS USB gadget
appeared at 13 seconds and then the system hung. So the kernel WAS REACHING user
space. A failure in of_clk_set_defaults() would have hung much earlier. What fits
that timeline is udev loading the module when the CCI bus finishes probing, with
the system already up.

With both fixes the node is genuinely inert until you modprobe.

With those two fixes the node is inert until you modprobe. But loading it brought
out another two, and the second is the worst of the lot:

3. The candidate GPIO list included 27 and 28, which are the i2c lines of CCI1
   itself. Since they were requested AS A BLOCK with
   devm_gpiod_get_array_optional(), a single owned pin aborted the whole sweep
   with -EINVAL. They are now requested one by one and the failing one is
   skipped, and the list comes from the phone's own ownership table instead of
   being guessed.

4. devm_regulator_bulk_get() KEEPS the pointer to the array it is handed and
   reads it again on release. I was passing it on probe()'s STACK, and since this
   probe always ends in -ENODEV, devres was freeing by reading dead memory: NULL
   deref inside devm_regulator_bulk_release. The kernel was left tainted and a
   while later UFS deadlocked while gating its clock (ufshcd_gate_work inside
   qmp_ufs_power_off, with ufshcd_hold waiting on it), leaving the phone with 35
   processes stuck waiting on disk and unable even to reboot cleanly. The array
   now goes on the heap with devm_kcalloc.

Of the four, number 4 is the one to remember: it did not blow up where it was
caused nor when it was caused.

